\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Macroeconomic Theory II: Homework 6
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem

\emph on
Planner's Problem
\emph default
: Consider the following problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t}(i),N_{t}(i)\}_{i\in[0,1],t=0,1,\ldots}}} & E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left(\frac{C_{t}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}-\frac{N_{t}^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}\right)\right\} 
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
C_{t}=\left[{\displaystyle \intop_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
N_{t}={\displaystyle \intop_{0}^{1}}N_{t}(i)di\\
C_{t}(i)=A_{t}N_{t}(i)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Notice that the allocation in this economy must be the first best, because
 the agent operates all the technologies by himself and maximizes the utility.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Solve this problem, and find optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compare what you find in (a) with the natural level of output we found in
 the lecture.
 Which is larger? What causes the difference?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To find the optimal 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

, we first maximize the consumption index with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

 subject to the second constraint.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max_{C_{t}(i)} & \left[{\displaystyle \intop_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & C_{t}(i)=A_{t}N_{t}(i)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The Lagrangian is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=\left[{\displaystyle \intop_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}+\lambda_{t}\left[A_{t}N_{t}(i)-C_{t}(i)\right]
\]

\end_inset

and the first-order condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
C_{t}(i)^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}} & = & \lambda_{t}\\
\left(\frac{C_{t}(i)}{C_{t}(j)}\right)^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}} & = & 1\\
C_{t}(i) & = & C_{t}(j)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

 is constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(i)=A_{t}N_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

 is also constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=N_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

, and hence, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(i)=A_{t}N_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}N_{t}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}=A_{t}N_{t}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now use this equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 as a constraint in the social planner's problem, which then becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\left\{ C_{t},N_{t}\right\} _{t=0}^{\infty}}} & E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left(\frac{C_{t}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}-\frac{N_{t}^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}\right)\right\} \\
\mbox{s.t.} & C_{t}=A_{t}N_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

After taking the derivative with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

, we obtain the following first-order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}^{-\sigma}A_{t}=\frac{N_{t}^{\varphi}}{1+\varphi}
\]

\end_inset

Plugging 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}=\nicefrac{C_{t}}{A_{t}}$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
C_{t}^{-\sigma}A_{t} & = & C_{t}^{\varphi}A_{t}^{-\varphi}\\
C_{t}^{\varphi+\sigma} & = & A_{t}^{1+\varphi}\\
C_{t}^{*} & = & A_{t}^{\frac{1+\varphi}{\varphi+\sigma}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which is the optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The natural level of output found in the lecture was
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}^{n}=Y_{t}^{n}=\left[\frac{1}{\mu}A_{t}^{1+\varphi}\right]^{\frac{1}{\varphi+\sigma}}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\mu}\equiv\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}<1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the natural level of output is less than the optimal level of output
 found in part A.
 This difference is obviously caused by the fictionless gross markup, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

.
 Each firm produces a differentiated good, so they wield monopoly power.
 As a result, they set a price higher than marginal cost, and produce less
 the socially optimal output to maximize profit.
 In contrast, the level of output found in part A is socially efficient
 because it solves the social planner's problem, which only maximizes total
 utility.
 Thus, due to markup power, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is less than the socially efficient level of output.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem

\emph on
Employment Subsidy
\emph default
.
 Now consider the following decentralized economy.
 Households solve the same problem as in the lecture:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left(\frac{C_{t}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}-\frac{N_{t}^{1-\varphi}}{1+\varphi}\right)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
{\displaystyle \intop_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)di+Q_{t}B_{t}=B_{t-1}+W_{t}N_{t}+T_{t}\\
C_{t}=\left[{\displaystyle \intop_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Prices are flexible.
 But now, firms receive employment subsidy:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{P_{t}(i)}}\left[P_{t}(i)\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}-\frac{(1-\tau)W_{t}}{A_{t}}\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}\right]
\]

\end_inset

i.e., a fraction 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 of wage is subsidized by the government.
 The outlays associated with this subsidy are financed by lump-sum taxes
 (that is, they are already included in 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{t}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Find equilibrium aggregate consumption/production level 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Compare the production level in (a) with the production level you found
 in the planner's problem.
 Which is larger if 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=0$
\end_inset

? Is there any subsidy level at which the production level of this economy
 coincides with the one in the planner's economy?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain intuitions behind your answers in (b).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we wish to maximize the consumption index 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 for any given level of expenditures 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{1}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

.
 The objective funciton of this maximization problem is known as the constant
 elasticity of substitution (CES) aggregator function.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t}(i)\}_{i\in[0,1]}}} & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)di=Z_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Lets setup the Lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}+\lambda\left[Z_{t}-{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)C_{t}(i)di\right]
\]

\end_inset

Important to note, in this construction, 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is just an index characterizing all the goods.
 Our goal is to optimize 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to a specific good (i.e., good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

).
 Thus, the first-order condition of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 with respect to good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial C_{t}(j)}=0\nonumber \\
\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{1}{\epsilon-1}}\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}C_{t}(i)^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}-\lambda P_{t}(i)=0\nonumber \\
\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{1}{\epsilon-1}}\left[C_{t}(j)\right]^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}=\lambda P_{t}(j);\ \forall\ j\in[0,1]\nonumber \\
\left(\frac{C_{t}(j)}{C_{t}(k)}\right)^{-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}=\left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(k)}\right);\ \forall\ k,j\in[0,1]
\end{gather}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From equation (1), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
C_{t}(j)=C_{t}(k)\left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(k)}\right)^{-\epsilon};\ \forall\ j,k\in[0,1]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Let us substitute equation (3) into the constraint.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(j)C_{t}(j)dj & = & Z_{t}\nonumber \\
{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(j)\left(\frac{P_{t}(j)}{P_{t}(k)}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}(k)dj & = & Z_{t}\nonumber \\
C_{t}(k)\left[P_{t}(k)\right]^{\epsilon}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left[P_{t}(j)\right]^{1-\epsilon}d_{j} & = & Z_{t}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Now, we define the Dixit-Stiglitx aggregate price index 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{t}\equiv\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(j)^{1-\epsilon}d_{j}\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{1-\epsilon}\equiv{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(j)^{1-\epsilon}d_{j}$
\end_inset

, then equation (4) becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
C_{t}(k)\left[P_{t}(k)\right]^{\epsilon}P_{t}^{1-\epsilon} & = & Z_{t}\nonumber \\
C_{t}(k) & = & \left(\frac{P_{t}(k)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

which is a necessary condition for maximizing the consumption index.
 Next, we substitute equation (6) into the consumption idex.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}C_{t}(k)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}dk\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}} & = & \left\{ {\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left[\left(\frac{P_{t}(k)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\left(\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)\right]^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}dk\right\} ^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left\{ \left[\left(\frac{1}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\left(\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)\right]^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(P_{t}(k)^{-\epsilon}\right)^{\frac{\epsilon-1}{\epsilon}}dk\right\} ^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\nonumber \\
 & = & \left(\frac{1}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\left(\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)\left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(k)^{1-\epsilon}dk\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Finally, from the aggregate price index, equation (7) yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\left(\frac{1}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}\left(\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)P_{t}^{-\epsilon}=\frac{Z_{t}}{P_{t}}
\end{gather}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, we have found the set of demand equations for each individual good,
 the maximized consumption index, and total consumption expenditure.
 With this information, the household no longer has to worry about choosing
 its consumption for each individual good.
 Instead, it chooses its consumption basket as a whole.
 Thus, its constrained maximization problem becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \max_{\{C_{t},B_{t},N_{t}\}}\ } & E_{0}\left\{ \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}\beta^{t}U(C_{t},N_{t})\right\} \\
\mbox{s.t.} & P_{t}C_{t}+Q_{t}B_{t}=B_{t-1}+W_{t}N_{t}+T_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

After setting up the Lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left[U(C_{t},N_{t})+\lambda_{t}\left(-P_{t}C_{t}-Q_{t}B_{t}+B_{t-1}+W_{t}N_{t}+T_{t}\right)\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

we obtain the following first-order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
U_{c,t}-\lambda_{t}P_{t} & = & 0\\
U_{n,t}+\lambda_{t}W_{t} & = & 0\\
\lambda_{t}(-Q_{t})+E_{t}\left\{ \beta\lambda_{t+1}\right\}  & = & 0
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equations (8) and (9) imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
-U_{n,t}=\left(\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)U_{c,t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Equations (8) and (10) imply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
Q_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \beta\frac{U_{c,t+1}}{U_{c,t}}\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t+1}}\right\} \nonumber \\
\frac{Q_{t}}{P_{t}}U_{c,t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \beta\frac{U_{c,t+1}}{P_{t+1}}\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under the assumption of a period utility given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U(C_{t},N_{t})=\frac{C_{t}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}-\frac{N_{t}^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the first-order conditions become
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
N_{t}^{\varphi} & = & \left(\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)C_{t}^{-\sigma}\\
Q_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \beta\left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t+1}}\right\} 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we move onto the firm's problem.
 Firm 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 reoptimizing in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 will choose its price 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}^{*}$
\end_inset

 that maximizes the current market value of its profits generated while
 that price remains effective.
 Formally, it solves the problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{P_{t}(i)}}\left[P_{t}(i)\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}-\frac{(1-\tau)W_{t}}{A_{t}}\left(\frac{P_{t}(i)}{P_{t}}\right)^{-\epsilon}C_{t}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The first-order condition associated with the problem above takes the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
(1-\epsilon)P_{t}(i)^{-\epsilon}P_{t}^{\epsilon}C_{t}+\epsilon\frac{(1-\tau)W_{t}}{A_{t}}P_{t}(i)^{-\epsilon-1}P_{t}^{\epsilon}C_{t} & = & 0\\
\epsilon\frac{(1-\tau)W_{t}}{A_{t}}P_{t}(i)^{-\epsilon-1} & = & (\epsilon-1)P_{t}(i)^{-\epsilon}\\
P_{t}(i) & = & \frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}\frac{(1-\tau)W_{t}}{A_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\equiv\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}>1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the aggregate price index becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P_{t} & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon}d_{j}\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}\\
 & = & \mu\frac{(1-\tau)W_{t}}{A_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In equilibrium, there are two market clearing conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Market clearing in the goods market requires
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{t}(i)=C_{t}(i)
\]

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

 and all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Letting aggregate output be defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}\equiv\left[\int_{0}^{1}Y_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}$
\end_inset

 it follows that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Y_{t}=C_{t}
\]

\end_inset

must hold for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Market clearing in the labor market requires
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
N_{t}={\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}N_{t}(i)di
\]

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since all the firms are symmetric and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}(i)=C_{t}(i)$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Y_{t} & = & \left[\int_{0}^{1}Y_{t}(i)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
 & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(A_{t}N_{t}(i)\right)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
 & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}\left(A_{t}N_{t}\right)^{1-\frac{1}{\epsilon}}di\right]^{\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}}\\
Y_{t} & = & A_{t}N_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Hence , the economy can be summarized by the following equations
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
N_{t}^{\varphi}=\left(\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)C_{t}^{-\sigma}\\
Q_{t}=E_{t}\left\{ \beta\left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t+1}}\right\} \\
P_{t}=\mu\frac{(1-\tau)W_{t}}{A_{t}}\\
Y_{t}=C_{t}\\
Y_{t}=A_{t}N_{t}\\
N_{t}={\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}N_{t}(i)di
\end{gather}

\end_inset

Solving for 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 in equation (16) and plugging it into equation (18) yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1=\mu\frac{(1-\tau)}{A_{t}}N_{t}^{\varphi}C_{t}^{\sigma}
\]

\end_inset

Then, using equations (19) and (20), we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1 & = & \mu\frac{(1-\tau)}{A_{t}}\left(\frac{Y_{t}}{A_{t}}\right)^{\varphi}Y_{t}^{\sigma}\\
Y_{t} & = & \left[\frac{1}{\mu}\frac{1}{(1-\tau)}A_{t}^{1+\varphi}\right]^{\frac{1}{\sigma+\varphi}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

This is the equilibrium aggregate consumption/production level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the social planner's problem, we found
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}^{*}=C_{t}^{P}=A_{t}^{\frac{1+\varphi}{\varphi+\sigma}}
\]

\end_inset

and in the employment subsidy problem,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}^{*}=C_{t}^{S}=\left[\frac{1}{\mu}\frac{1}{(1-\tau)}A_{t}^{1+\varphi}\right]^{\frac{1}{\sigma+\varphi}}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}^{S}>C_{t}^{P}$
\end_inset

 when
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\frac{1}{\mu}\frac{1}{(1-\tau)}\right]^{\frac{1}{\varphi+\sigma}}>1\implies\mu(1-\tau)<1
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}^{P}>C_{t}^{S}$
\end_inset

 when
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu(1-\tau)>1
\]

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}^{S}=C_{t}^{P}$
\end_inset

 when
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu(1-\tau)=1
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=0$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{t}^{S}=\left[\frac{1}{\mu}A_{t}^{1+\varphi}\right]^{\frac{1}{\sigma+\varphi}}
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\equiv\frac{\epsilon}{\epsilon-1}>1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}^{P}>C_{t}^{S}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the subsidized level of output is lower than the social planner's
 level of output when 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As mentioned earlier, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(1-\tau)=1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}^{S}=C_{t}^{P}$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tau=\frac{\mu-1}{\mu}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=0$
\end_inset

, then wage is no longer subsidized by the government.
 In this case, output level is the same as the natural level of output when
 firms sell differentiated goods and prices are flexible.
 As 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 increases, the firms are willing to hire more workers since the government
 is subsidizing wages.
 This, in turn, leads to an increase in output.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, consider the case when 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=\frac{\mu-1}{\mu}$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\tau)=\frac{1}{\mu}$
\end_inset

.
 Using the employment subsidy condition, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P_{t}(i) & = & \mu\frac{(1-\tau)}{A_{t}}W_{t}\\
 & = & \frac{W_{t}}{A_{t}}\\
P_{t} & = & \left[{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{1}}P_{t}(i)^{1-\epsilon}d_{j}\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\epsilon}}\\
 & = & \frac{W_{t}}{A_{t}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The cost of one unit of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 and each unit of 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 can produce 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

 unites of 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{W_{t}}{A_{t}}$
\end_inset

 is simply the marginal cost of 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that for 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=\frac{\mu-1}{\mu}$
\end_inset

, aggregate price 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}$
\end_inset

 equals the marginal cost to produce 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we have a competitive equilibrium.
 Since the utility function is locally non-satiated, then the ouput level
 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}^{S}$
\end_inset

 is efficient by the First Fundamental Welfare Theorem of Economics.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}^{S}=C_{t}^{P}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

